./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1159:\begin{example} \rm \label{ex:223withQIP}
./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1160:  For $\ud=(2,2,3)$ the \eqref{eq:QIP} is minimizing the function $G_{\ud}(\ue)=e_1^2+e_1e_2+e_2^2+e_2$ on the three-element set $\{(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)\}$. The minimum is 4, attained at the second and third element of the set. Hence $\theta=2$, $C=4$, cf. Examples \ref{ex:222324}, \ref{ex:q-series}.  
./paper-sources/lehalleur-rimanyi-2024-geometry-of-dln-fibre/source/main.tex:1164:  For $\ud=(8,8,11,11,11,13,13,13,15)$ the minimal value of \eqref{eq:QIP} is 55, and it is achieved at four points: $e=(4,2,1,1,0,0,0,0)$, $e=(4,1,2,1,0,0,0,0)$, $e=(4,1,1,2,0,0,0,0)$, $e=(5,1,1,1,0,0,0,0)$. %We will use this dimension vector $\ud$ to illustrate some combinatorial phenomena below (cf. Figure \ref{fig:largeLD}).
